# Read-Only Assurance Review Protocol and Report

> Use only for a review explicitly authorized as read-only. This protocol does
> not authorize network access, project hooks, refresh, generation, package
> installation, or any external-system query. If a required check is not
> demonstrably read-only, mark it unavailable or skipped.

## Review boundary

- Subject and scope:
- Reviewer role:
- Start time and environment:
- Authorized read-only commands:
- Explicit exclusions:
- External systems in scope: none unless separately authorized read-only access
  is recorded here.

## Before-review baseline

Record, without refreshing generated artifacts:

- Exact repository path:
- `GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0 git status --short --branch` output and exit status:
- `GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0 git rev-parse --verify HEAD` output and exit status:
- Existing tracked, untracked, and ignored drift:
- Fingerprints of consequential authority, register, gate, handoff, and
  generated-projection files where useful:
- Known stale generated projections:
- Unavailable fingerprint or repository probes:

The baseline distinguishes pre-existing drift from changes caused by the
review. A clean status is not required, but it must be disclosed.

## Command log

Run only commands whose current project contract explicitly declares them
read-only. `./octon check` and source inspection do not run project hooks or
refresh projections. Do not run `refresh`, `maintain ... --apply`, the
project-check evidence writer, build/test commands with unknown side effects,
or generators.

| Sequence | Exact argv/command | Declared read-only basis | Started/ended | Exit status | Result | Unavailable/skipped reason | Observed side effects |
|---:|---|---|---|---:|---|---|---|
|  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |

For a validation failure caused by stale projections, report:

- authoritative input that changed;
- stale output affected;
- validator finding;
- safe explicit refresh route; and
- why no rewrite occurred during this review.

Do not remove a legitimate source from fingerprint or integrity scope merely
to make the check pass.

## Evidence interpretation

State separately:

- structural validation performed and result;
- executable implementation evidence inspected;
- operational evidence inspected;
- specialist evidence inspected;
- checks unavailable or skipped;
- evidence freshness and subject fingerprint; and
- what the evidence does not prove.

A structural pass does not establish executable implementation, specialist
approval, release readiness, production readiness, product efficacy, or
commercial viability.

## Separate conclusions

Use `supported`, `partially supported`, `not supported`, `not assessed`, or
`not applicable`. Each row has its own evidence and limitations; do not infer
one row from another.

| Conclusion | Status | Basis and evidence | Limitations |
|---|---|---|---|
| Architecture quality |  |  |  |
| Documentation completeness |  |  |  |
| Implementation evidence |  |  |  |
| Specialist approval |  |  |  |
| Release readiness |  |  |  |
| Production readiness |  |  |  |
| Product efficacy or commercial viability |  |  |  |

## After-review baseline

Repeat the same probes and fingerprints used before review:

- Final repository status and revision:
- Final fingerprints:
- Before/after differences:
- Files created, modified, deleted, or metadata-changed:
- External systems changed:
- Caches, bytecode, lockfiles, reports, indexes, or timestamps created:

If any change occurred, do not claim the review was read-only. Identify
whether the change was pre-existing, caused by an executed command, or remains
unknown, and preserve evidence for remediation.

